        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 542,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 504,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71489,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71490,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastatic tumours can affect any part of the kidney.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71491,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastatic tumours manifesting as renal masses are rare. Metastases that are biopsied or resected frequently occur as an isolated renal mass mimicking a primary renal tumour. A new renal tumour in a patient with a history of a non-renal malignancy that has not progressed is nearly always a new primary renal tumour; in contrast, in a patient with a progressive non-renal malignancy, it may be a metastasis {15470708}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71492,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All types of malignancies have been reported to metastasize to the kidney, affecting both male and female patients with a wide age range {25406592}. As many as 10% of renal masses evaluated in one centre were metastatic tumours {10405806}; however, the overall frequency of non-renal metastases manifesting as renal masses is probably &lt;&nbsp;1% {25406592}. Rarely, a non-renal metastasis may metastasize to a primary renal tumour such as an angiomyolipoma, oncocytoma, or renal cell carcinoma {22754705; 23891136; 10636586}. In nearly 90% of cases, the primary tumour is known before a diagnosis of metastatic tumour to the kidney is made, and rarely is the diagnosis of the primary tumour made by evaluation of the renal mass {25406592}. Most metastatic tumours (&gt;&nbsp;80%) are carcinomas, and lung is the most frequent primary, but non-epithelial tumours have been reported as well as multiple primary tumour types {25406592}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71493,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71494,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most metastases to the kidney are believed to occur through haematogenous spread. Secondary involvement by direct extension, such as from a retroperitoneal sarcoma, may also occur.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71495,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours typically manifest as a solid mass with a variable appearance, but occasionally metastases can be cystic {25406592}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71496,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histopathological features reflect the primary origin of the tumour. There is usually locoregional spread of the tumour, with involvement of the renal sinus fat or perinephric fat. Thyroid carcinomas of all types can metastasize to the kidney and must be distinguished from thyroid-like follicular carcinoma of the kidney {19047894}. Thyroid-like follicular carcinoma is non-reactive for thyroid transcription factor&nbsp;1 (TTF1) and thyroglobulin, whereas metastatic thyroid carcinoma should be positive for these proteins. PAX8 can be positive in both. Tumour-to-tumour metastases may occasionally involve primary benign and malignant renal tumours {10636586; 33537247}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71497,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytological examination of a renal mass may be useful in identifying a metastatic tumour, particularly in patients with another known primary tumour, and may avoid unnecessary surgery {10405806; 26053895}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71498,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71499,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a renal mass with morphological features of a non-renal site of origin.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> a known history of the primary malignancy; confirmatory immunohistochemistry.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71500,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases to the kidney are staged using the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) system according to the primary tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71501,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 290,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis is dependent on the type and extent of the primary malignancy.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T17:19:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]